1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,610
In nineteen sixty-three, the United
States Army was losing space inside

2
00:00:04,625 --> 00:00:07,580
a city it had built
beneath Greenland's ice.

3
00:00:07,925 --> 00:00:10,080
Not to an enemy. Not to an avalanche.

4
00:00:10,585 --> 00:00:13,080
The ceiling itself was slowly coming down.

5
00:00:13,585 --> 00:00:17,290
This was Camp Century: a working
settlement hidden beneath the

6
00:00:17,305 --> 00:00:19,240
surface of the Greenland ice sheet.

7
00:00:19,725 --> 00:00:22,960
There were sleeping quarters,
workshops, medical facilities,

8
00:00:23,205 --> 00:00:26,020
heated corridors,
and a nuclear power plant.

9
00:00:26,525 --> 00:00:29,540
Tracked vehicles could travel
along an underground main street.

10
00:00:29,825 --> 00:00:32,520
But the Army's own measurements
told another story.

11
00:00:33,405 --> 00:00:37,350
The Army documented one section
of its main passage losing roughly

12
00:00:37,365 --> 00:00:40,440
twelve percent
of its cross-sectional area per year.

13
00:00:40,845 --> 00:00:44,340
In a separate side passage,
the figure was seventeen percent.

14
00:00:44,965 --> 00:00:48,940
Those were measured examples, not
identical rates for every tunnel.

15
00:00:49,105 --> 00:00:53,590
The Army's engineers estimated that
keeping this one camp's tunnels

16
00:00:53,605 --> 00:00:58,170
at their original dimensions would
require cutting and removing around

17
00:00:58,185 --> 00:01:01,120
twenty thousand cubic
yards of snow every year.

18
00:01:01,545 --> 00:01:05,300
That was an estimate, not
a record of snow actually removed.

19
00:01:05,565 --> 00:01:08,320
The Army's surviving film
records the machines,

20
00:01:08,585 --> 00:01:11,180
trenches, and extraordinary
construction effort.

21
00:01:11,565 --> 00:01:15,150
The published engineering report
shows how the arches and rooms were

22
00:01:15,165 --> 00:01:16,780
assembled beneath the surface.

23
00:01:17,285 --> 00:01:20,570
Everything looks finished. But
the biggest construction job might

24
00:01:20,585 --> 00:01:22,252
have been the one that never ended.

25
00:01:22,525 --> 00:01:25,520
Now for the part the public
movie did not tell in full.

26
00:01:25,845 --> 00:01:29,590
As the research program developed,
military planners proposed a much

27
00:01:29,605 --> 00:01:33,430
larger system: buried railways
and launching locations for

28
00:01:33,445 --> 00:01:35,220
hundreds of nuclear-armed missiles.

29
00:01:35,385 --> 00:01:36,960
The arsenal remained a proposal.

30
00:01:37,285 --> 00:01:41,290
The base was real. The mystery
isn't whether the Army could build

31
00:01:41,305 --> 00:01:43,640
beneath the ice. It clearly could.

32
00:01:44,005 --> 00:01:47,510
The question is why building one
working city did not lead to the

33
00:01:47,525 --> 00:01:50,220
enormous underground military
system being imagined.

34
00:01:50,545 --> 00:01:53,990
And the first clue lies in a journey
of one hundred thirty-eight miles,

35
00:01:54,005 --> 00:01:56,000
through a place without a road.

36
00:01:56,705 --> 00:01:58,229
The year is nineteen fifty-nine.

37
00:01:58,965 --> 00:02:02,590
On the northwest Greenland ice
sheet, there is no construction site

38
00:02:02,605 --> 00:02:04,540
as most people would recognize one.

39
00:02:04,985 --> 00:02:07,740
There is no paved roadway
leading to a construction gate.

40
00:02:08,045 --> 00:02:09,680
The buildings do not exist yet.

41
00:02:10,065 --> 00:02:12,320
There is no paved road
leading to the site.

42
00:02:13,105 --> 00:02:16,310
Greenland is part of the Danish
realm, and the United States operates

43
00:02:16,325 --> 00:02:18,277
in the region under defense arrangements.

44
00:02:18,805 --> 00:02:21,060
Geography, however, has its own rules.

45
00:02:21,345 --> 00:02:25,680
The project uses Camp Tuto, a staging
area near the edge of the ice.

46
00:02:26,025 --> 00:02:29,480
From there, men and machinery
must travel far inland.

47
00:02:29,705 --> 00:02:32,870
The distance to Camp Century is
approximately one hundred thirty-eight

48
00:02:32,885 --> 00:02:36,840
miles. Tractors pull long
sled trains across the snow,

49
00:02:37,085 --> 00:02:40,180
carrying the materials needed
to create an underground settlement.

50
00:02:40,665 --> 00:02:44,530
Some loads are so awkward that
the Army has to make special sleds

51
00:02:44,545 --> 00:02:48,660
for them. It is the image that makes
the construction story so tangible:

52
00:02:48,945 --> 00:02:52,710
a small line of heavy machines
moving across an almost featureless

53
00:02:52,725 --> 00:02:56,740
white horizon. But the cargo tells
you what is really happening.

54
00:02:57,745 --> 00:03:01,680
Steel, timber, prefabricated rooms,
fuel, and heavy electrical equipment.

55
00:03:02,285 --> 00:03:05,830
In the first season, roughly one
thousand short tons of materials

56
00:03:05,845 --> 00:03:08,200
move from Camp Tuto toward the site.

57
00:03:08,625 --> 00:03:12,160
In nineteen sixty, the total is
another four thousand six hundred.

58
00:03:12,705 --> 00:03:16,840
About five thousand six hundred short
tons in two construction seasons.

59
00:03:17,025 --> 00:03:20,920
And every machine hauling that
cargo needs fuel of its own.

60
00:03:21,565 --> 00:03:23,580
Spare parts have to follow the same route.

61
00:03:23,945 --> 00:03:27,730
When conditions change, a trip can
become another test of the transport

62
00:03:27,745 --> 00:03:31,610
system. This is the first hidden cost
of building in the Arctic: before

63
00:03:31,625 --> 00:03:34,650
your machines can solve the problem,
you must build a way to bring

64
00:03:34,665 --> 00:03:37,730
the machines there. Yet
by October nineteen sixty,

65
00:03:37,745 --> 00:03:40,300
the Army has created a functioning camp.

66
00:03:40,845 --> 00:03:42,440
What came next was even stranger.

67
00:03:43,065 --> 00:03:45,303
They did not blast their
way through a mountain.

68
00:03:45,445 --> 00:03:48,840
They did not drill an enormous
tunnel through solid glacial ice.

69
00:03:49,385 --> 00:03:53,800
They took advantage of something easier to
cut than rock. And much harder to trust.

70
00:03:54,565 --> 00:03:58,350
The central construction machine at
Camp Century was called the Peter

71
00:03:58,365 --> 00:04:02,870
snow miller. In the Army's period
images, this machine resembles an

72
00:04:02,885 --> 00:04:06,920
industrial cutter trying to eat
its way through an endless white wall.

73
00:04:07,265 --> 00:04:09,740
It cuts a long trench into compacted snow.

74
00:04:10,325 --> 00:04:14,180
The next step is deceptively
simple: build inside the trench,

75
00:04:14,365 --> 00:04:17,400
cover it with a roof,
then put snow back on top.

76
00:04:17,825 --> 00:04:19,640
This is cut-and-cover construction.

77
00:04:19,925 --> 00:04:23,510
But unlike an ordinary city,
the material above and beside the

78
00:04:23,525 --> 00:04:25,260
buildings is not stable earth.

79
00:04:25,665 --> 00:04:29,540
It's snow and firn, constantly
responding to pressure and temperature.

80
00:04:29,925 --> 00:04:31,980
The Army tested different forms of roof.

81
00:04:32,445 --> 00:04:35,650
In some locations, the trench
walls were undercut so that

82
00:04:35,665 --> 00:04:37,680
compacted snow could form an arch.

83
00:04:37,965 --> 00:04:41,500
In others, metal arches helped
support the larger openings.

84
00:04:41,585 --> 00:04:44,570
The engineers were trying to make
the surrounding snow part of the

85
00:04:44,585 --> 00:04:47,620
structure, without allowing
it to destroy the structure.

86
00:04:48,105 --> 00:04:51,590
For the broad reactor building space,
the Army needed a trench around

87
00:04:51,605 --> 00:04:56,500
forty feet wide. That width brought
new structural loads and new risks.

88
00:04:57,265 --> 00:04:59,600
Some of the first failures
were quietly revealing.

89
00:05:00,285 --> 00:05:04,110
In certain sections, temporary
roof forms were removed before the

90
00:05:04,125 --> 00:05:06,125
snow above them had properly strengthened.

91
00:05:06,625 --> 00:05:08,320
The openings began to deform.

92
00:05:08,805 --> 00:05:12,200
The solution was sometimes to leave
the support forms where they were.

93
00:05:12,425 --> 00:05:15,490
These compromises would matter
long after the promotional film was

94
00:05:15,505 --> 00:05:17,934
finished. But the buildings
were only the beginning.

95
00:05:18,325 --> 00:05:21,870
Under the snow, Camp Century
developed a main street and a network

96
00:05:21,885 --> 00:05:25,220
of smaller passages. The layout
included a dining hall,

97
00:05:25,465 --> 00:05:28,960
barracks, a medical
facility, workshops, storage,

98
00:05:29,785 --> 00:05:31,928
recreational rooms, even
a chapel and theater.

99
00:05:32,285 --> 00:05:35,060
The camp was designed for as
many as two hundred people.

100
00:05:35,285 --> 00:05:37,660
From the outside, a blank field of snow.

101
00:05:37,845 --> 00:05:39,960
Inside, an operating settlement.

102
00:05:40,365 --> 00:05:42,100
There was one important compromise.

103
00:05:42,385 --> 00:05:44,800
Every roof carried
the weight of snow above it.

104
00:05:44,945 --> 00:05:48,250
And the camp would be adding something
that made the surrounding environment

105
00:05:48,265 --> 00:05:50,020
even more complicated. Heat.

106
00:05:50,605 --> 00:05:53,710
Not just from the people living
there, but from a machine that cost

107
00:05:53,725 --> 00:05:55,680
more than most of the city around it.

108
00:05:56,345 --> 00:05:59,200
The Army had a supply
problem it could not escape.

109
00:05:59,705 --> 00:06:02,410
Keep a remote settlement
warm with diesel generators,

110
00:06:02,425 --> 00:06:06,160
and every season brings another
long convoy carrying fuel.

111
00:06:06,585 --> 00:06:09,970
Or install a machine that can produce
a large amount of power without

112
00:06:09,985 --> 00:06:12,080
the same continuous fuel deliveries.

113
00:06:12,625 --> 00:06:16,910
That second idea became PM-two-A,
Camp Century's portable nuclear

114
00:06:16,925 --> 00:06:19,000
power plant. It wasn't a bomb.

115
00:06:19,285 --> 00:06:23,470
It was an electrical and heating system
for the camp. The price was extraordinary.

116
00:06:23,485 --> 00:06:28,170
A nineteen sixty-five Army report gives
Camp Century's actual construction

117
00:06:28,185 --> 00:06:30,930
cost as seven point nine
two million dollars.

118
00:06:31,145 --> 00:06:34,730
The nuclear power plant alone
accounted for about five point seven

119
00:06:34,745 --> 00:06:37,260
million. Those are period dollars.

120
00:06:37,445 --> 00:06:40,550
And they mean that the plant consumed
the great majority of the initial

121
00:06:40,565 --> 00:06:44,610
project cost. The reactor arrived
as another set of heavy loads hauled

122
00:06:44,625 --> 00:06:49,050
across the ice. Engineers had to assemble
it in the prepared underground

123
00:06:49,065 --> 00:06:53,820
plant space. Its reactor produced
heat on the scale of ten megawatts,

124
00:06:54,065 --> 00:06:57,570
while the electrical output supplied
to the camp was roughly one and

125
00:06:57,585 --> 00:07:01,000
a half megawatts. Those figures
measure different things.

126
00:07:01,425 --> 00:07:04,758
Reactor heat is not the same as
electricity delivered to the buildings.

127
00:07:05,025 --> 00:07:09,660
At Camp Century, the heat itself
was useful. The base required water.

128
00:07:10,185 --> 00:07:14,170
Engineers developed systems to melt
snow and ice and recover usable

129
00:07:14,185 --> 00:07:16,020
water from beneath the surface.

130
00:07:16,425 --> 00:07:17,870
But this creates another problem.

131
00:07:17,885 --> 00:07:20,800
Where do you put the water
after people have used it?

132
00:07:21,065 --> 00:07:24,480
The Army's engineers built
underground disposal sumps.

133
00:07:24,625 --> 00:07:28,170
One was placed too near living
quarters during a rush to finish before

134
00:07:28,185 --> 00:07:30,900
winter. The odors reached
the occupied buildings.

135
00:07:31,345 --> 00:07:33,300
And that wasn't the worst consequence.

136
00:07:34,305 --> 00:07:37,360
Warm liquid moving into the snow
changed the ground nearby.

137
00:07:37,685 --> 00:07:41,350
The original report described the floor
near the disposal area settling

138
00:07:41,365 --> 00:07:45,310
by roughly four feet, forcing
the removal of two nearby buildings.

139
00:07:45,325 --> 00:07:49,290
The camp had literally altered
the material holding up part of its

140
00:07:49,305 --> 00:07:53,270
own infrastructure. Meanwhile,
the nuclear plant's turbine-generator

141
00:07:53,285 --> 00:07:55,820
developed repeated mechanical troubles.

142
00:07:55,985 --> 00:07:59,510
Keeping the city supplied meant
solving ordinary industrial failures

143
00:07:59,525 --> 00:08:01,460
in a profoundly unusual place.

144
00:08:01,845 --> 00:08:04,260
The reactor was a real
technical achievement.

145
00:08:04,485 --> 00:08:06,780
But the more systems
the Army put underground,

146
00:08:07,145 --> 00:08:08,621
the more there was to maintain.

147
00:08:09,245 --> 00:08:13,110
The reactor had been planned before
Project Iceworm was formally proposed.

148
00:08:13,125 --> 00:08:17,770
Its cost cannot be treated as proof
that the later missile plan financed

149
00:08:17,785 --> 00:08:22,150
the camp. But the working research
camp made an extraordinary idea

150
00:08:22,165 --> 00:08:26,690
seem tangible: use buried infrastructure
to conceal launching positions

151
00:08:26,705 --> 00:08:29,220
that would not always
stay in the same place.

152
00:08:29,995 --> 00:08:32,190
The Army made films about Camp Century.

153
00:08:32,655 --> 00:08:36,050
The cameras showed construction
equipment, comfortable rooms,

154
00:08:36,435 --> 00:08:39,450
Arctic research, and an American
engineering triumph.

155
00:08:39,995 --> 00:08:42,630
Much of that was true. The camp was real.

156
00:08:42,875 --> 00:08:44,530
The scientific work was real.

157
00:08:44,855 --> 00:08:47,370
The challenges of living
beneath the ice were real.

158
00:08:47,595 --> 00:08:51,100
But military research connected
it to another proposal that the

159
00:08:51,115 --> 00:08:53,230
public film did not fully explain.

160
00:08:53,845 --> 00:08:58,280
Project Iceworm. The idea was to use
Greenland's ice sheet to conceal

161
00:08:58,295 --> 00:09:01,690
a vast network of mobile
ballistic missile positions.

162
00:09:01,955 --> 00:09:04,030
A fixed missile base can be mapped.

163
00:09:04,275 --> 00:09:07,020
A missile moved through
an underground network would be harder

164
00:09:07,035 --> 00:09:09,190
to locate, at least in theory.

165
00:09:10,015 --> 00:09:13,320
Historical research describes plans
for roughly six hundred missiles

166
00:09:13,335 --> 00:09:16,730
and more than two thousand
possible launching positions.

167
00:09:16,855 --> 00:09:19,870
Not six hundred missiles
actually delivered to Greenland.

168
00:09:20,455 --> 00:09:23,270
Not two thousand completed
underground silos.

169
00:09:23,455 --> 00:09:25,410
Those numbers describe a proposal.

170
00:09:25,695 --> 00:09:29,380
Historical research describes
a proposed network across approximately

171
00:09:29,395 --> 00:09:31,950
one hundred thirty-five
thousand square kilometers.

172
00:09:32,255 --> 00:09:35,160
And the Army had one experimental
component that made the

173
00:09:35,175 --> 00:09:37,110
proposal sound less imaginary.

174
00:09:37,555 --> 00:09:41,380
The Army had installed an experimental
subsurface railway at Camp

175
00:09:41,395 --> 00:09:44,530
Century, but this was only
a research-scale installation.

176
00:09:44,975 --> 00:09:48,500
That was real. But a short
experimental track is not a functioning

177
00:09:48,515 --> 00:09:50,990
continent-sized missile transport network.

178
00:09:51,175 --> 00:09:53,060
Every passage would have to remain open.

179
00:09:53,075 --> 00:09:55,370
Every point would require maintenance.

180
00:09:55,875 --> 00:09:59,760
Trains, machinery, power,
communications, and crews would all need

181
00:09:59,775 --> 00:10:03,360
support. And those demands would
be multiplied thousands of times

182
00:10:03,375 --> 00:10:05,390
over. There was another obstacle.

183
00:10:05,895 --> 00:10:09,300
The United States was building on
Greenland, within the Kingdom of

184
00:10:09,315 --> 00:10:13,660
Denmark. An American research
installation did not automatically provide

185
00:10:13,675 --> 00:10:17,470
political consent for an enormous
hidden nuclear weapons complex.

186
00:10:17,975 --> 00:10:21,740
Within the U.S. military, the Air
Force and Navy were also developing

187
00:10:21,755 --> 00:10:23,750
other strategic missile systems.

188
00:10:24,275 --> 00:10:28,600
The Army proposal faced rival
technologies, questions about its military

189
00:10:28,615 --> 00:10:32,230
purpose, and problems no engineering
demonstration could resolve.

190
00:10:32,535 --> 00:10:36,340
According to a later scientific
study, the proposal was rejected in

191
00:10:36,355 --> 00:10:39,870
nineteen sixty-three. The missile
network was never completed,

192
00:10:40,175 --> 00:10:42,090
and the weapons were never deployed.

193
00:10:42,135 --> 00:10:45,370
But even if a government had decided
to finance the entire scheme,

194
00:10:45,655 --> 00:10:47,650
the base had already produced a warning.

195
00:10:47,835 --> 00:10:50,320
Cutting the tunnels would
have been only the beginning.

196
00:10:50,335 --> 00:10:53,080
Keeping a vast network usable
would be another challenge,

197
00:10:53,095 --> 00:10:55,470
alongside the military
and political objections.

198
00:10:56,285 --> 00:11:00,430
By the early nineteen sixties, Camp
Century's engineers were measuring

199
00:11:00,445 --> 00:11:02,683
something their cameras
couldn't make glamorous.

200
00:11:03,105 --> 00:11:04,760
The shrinking of empty space.

201
00:11:05,265 --> 00:11:07,600
A snow trench can look perfectly solid.

202
00:11:08,125 --> 00:11:10,400
Then, month by month, the roof settles.

203
00:11:10,825 --> 00:11:14,730
The walls creep inward because the snow
and firn around them are deforming

204
00:11:14,745 --> 00:11:17,600
under pressure. The Army knew about creep.

205
00:11:17,945 --> 00:11:20,300
This wasn't the discovery
of a new physical law.

206
00:11:20,505 --> 00:11:23,890
The problem was the scale of what
maintaining a city inside that

207
00:11:23,905 --> 00:11:25,830
moving material actually required.

208
00:11:25,845 --> 00:11:29,840
Look at two diagrams in the Army's
nineteen sixty-five report.

209
00:11:30,305 --> 00:11:32,900
They show the same tunnel
sections at different dates.

210
00:11:33,485 --> 00:11:35,770
The difference isn't
subtle. In the main passage,

211
00:11:35,785 --> 00:11:39,210
the report illustrates one section
losing about twelve percent of

212
00:11:39,225 --> 00:11:41,080
its cross-sectional area a year.

213
00:11:41,145 --> 00:11:44,510
A side passage is documented
at roughly seventeen percent.

214
00:11:44,525 --> 00:11:48,790
The Army also reported average annual
vertical closure of about sixteen

215
00:11:48,805 --> 00:11:52,510
inches in the main passage, and about
thirty-four inches in side trenches.

216
00:11:52,525 --> 00:11:57,120
These are clearance losses, not
additional percentages of floor area.

217
00:11:57,585 --> 00:11:59,460
Not every trench behaved identically.

218
00:12:00,005 --> 00:12:03,560
Heat, geometry, supports,
and snow conditions all mattered.

219
00:12:04,145 --> 00:12:07,050
But even the average figures
turn a finished city into a

220
00:12:07,065 --> 00:12:08,460
continuing construction site.

221
00:12:08,725 --> 00:12:11,740
A corridor must be tall enough
for its occupants and equipment.

222
00:12:12,225 --> 00:12:15,780
When it loses height, someone
has to create that space again.

223
00:12:16,445 --> 00:12:18,890
Maintaining the original
openings throughout the camp,

224
00:12:18,905 --> 00:12:22,739
the Army estimated, would mean
trimming and removing roughly twenty

225
00:12:22,754 --> 00:12:24,960
thousand cubic yards of snow per year.

226
00:12:25,005 --> 00:12:28,750
This was a maintenance estimate,
not a claim that twenty thousand

227
00:12:28,765 --> 00:12:31,940
cubic yards were actually
hauled out every year.

228
00:12:32,525 --> 00:12:36,260
Still, picture the implication.
You build the city once.

229
00:12:36,665 --> 00:12:40,860
Then you assign machinery and crews
to keep rebuilding its empty spaces.

230
00:12:41,405 --> 00:12:43,360
And the roof is not the only problem.

231
00:12:43,705 --> 00:12:45,660
Blowing snow buried access ramps.

232
00:12:46,305 --> 00:12:49,280
Warm air affected parts
of the surrounding structure.

233
00:12:49,705 --> 00:12:51,860
Some forms had been removed too early.

234
00:12:52,645 --> 00:12:55,480
Systems inside the camp
produced heat, moisture,

235
00:12:55,765 --> 00:12:57,740
and maintenance demands of their own.

236
00:12:57,965 --> 00:13:01,230
Figure thirty-three of the report
shows a soldier using an electric

237
00:13:01,245 --> 00:13:05,120
chain saw to cut snow away from
the eave of a building inside a trench.

238
00:13:05,545 --> 00:13:07,070
It looks like an ordinary maintenance

239
00:13:07,085 --> 00:13:09,450
task, until you remember
what is above the workers.

240
00:13:09,465 --> 00:13:13,130
More snow, under pressure,
settling every season.

241
00:13:13,145 --> 00:13:16,740
It is one thing to accept that
burden for a scientific test camp.

242
00:13:17,185 --> 00:13:20,630
It is quite another to accept it
for a dispersed strategic missile

243
00:13:20,645 --> 00:13:23,020
force that must remain ready when needed.

244
00:13:23,765 --> 00:13:26,360
Camp Century demonstrated
a way to build in the snow.

245
00:13:26,845 --> 00:13:29,670
Its measurements showed why making
that way permanent would be so

246
00:13:29,685 --> 00:13:32,950
hard. And now we reach the part
of the story where the Army had to

247
00:13:32,965 --> 00:13:35,000
decide what the experiment
was still worth.

248
00:13:35,695 --> 00:13:39,010
Camp Century did not end
in a single cinematic collapse.

249
00:13:39,535 --> 00:13:41,890
Instead, its purpose began to fade.

250
00:13:42,535 --> 00:13:44,610
The Iceworm proposal was rejected.

251
00:13:45,175 --> 00:13:48,830
The nuclear power plant was shut
down in July nineteen sixty-three,

252
00:13:49,435 --> 00:13:52,050
then dismantled and removed
the following year.

253
00:13:52,695 --> 00:13:56,640
One scientific history dates the end
of year-round occupation to summer

254
00:13:56,655 --> 00:14:00,450
nineteen sixty-three, while another
places it in nineteen sixty-four.

255
00:14:00,975 --> 00:14:04,890
Seasonal work continued into the later
sixties; one study dates final

256
00:14:04,905 --> 00:14:06,660
abandonment to nineteen sixty-seven.

257
00:14:07,165 --> 00:14:08,900
Then Camp Century was abandoned.

258
00:14:09,045 --> 00:14:12,230
The Army had proved that an underground
Arctic settlement could be

259
00:14:12,245 --> 00:14:15,850
constructed. But the larger weapons
system had failed to justify a

260
00:14:15,865 --> 00:14:17,700
path from experiment to deployment.

261
00:14:18,065 --> 00:14:21,610
And shutting down a place under
snow is not the same as making it

262
00:14:21,625 --> 00:14:24,060
vanish. The reactor
hardware was taken out.

263
00:14:24,505 --> 00:14:26,840
The entire buried infrastructure was not.

264
00:14:28,005 --> 00:14:30,815
Materials, structures, and wastes
remained in the ice sheet.

265
00:14:31,225 --> 00:14:34,510
For a long time, the assumption
was that snowfall would continue to

266
00:14:34,525 --> 00:14:36,340
seal the abandoned site away.

267
00:14:37,085 --> 00:14:40,650
Decades later, scientists began
asking whether that assumption would

268
00:14:40,665 --> 00:14:43,340
remain reliable as
Greenland's climate changed.

269
00:14:43,865 --> 00:14:47,610
One study in twenty sixteen modeled
the possibility that surface loss

270
00:14:47,625 --> 00:14:51,530
of ice could eventually expose
or remobilize buried material

271
00:14:51,545 --> 00:14:53,560
under a high-emissions scenario.

272
00:14:53,965 --> 00:14:57,830
But this was a scenario, not
an observation of a radioactive river

273
00:14:57,845 --> 00:15:00,870
running into the sea. The United
States Government Accountability

274
00:15:00,885 --> 00:15:05,030
Office reported in twenty twenty-four
that a twenty twenty-one study

275
00:15:05,045 --> 00:15:09,430
expected the buried contamination
to remain largely immobile through

276
00:15:09,445 --> 00:15:13,890
the year twenty-one hundred. So
the scientific question is not resolved

277
00:15:13,905 --> 00:15:15,800
by a single dramatic headline.

278
00:15:16,925 --> 00:15:19,560
Denmark established permanent
ice-sheet monitoring.

279
00:15:20,165 --> 00:15:23,310
Different projections depend on
their assumptions about Greenland's

280
00:15:23,325 --> 00:15:26,440
future climate. And there
is one last twist.

281
00:15:27,065 --> 00:15:31,100
The Army went to Greenland to learn how
to build inside ice that was moving.

282
00:15:31,485 --> 00:15:35,130
When the soldiers left, some
of the most valuable discoveries came

283
00:15:35,145 --> 00:15:38,470
not from the buildings they abandoned,
but from what researchers had

284
00:15:38,485 --> 00:15:40,420
drilled out of the ground beneath them.

285
00:15:40,925 --> 00:15:44,150
While Camp Century disappeared
from view, something much older

286
00:15:44,165 --> 00:15:45,460
was waiting to be found.

287
00:15:46,115 --> 00:15:49,880
In nineteen sixty-six, scientific
drilling at Camp Century reached

288
00:15:49,895 --> 00:15:51,830
the bottom of the Greenland ice sheet.

289
00:15:52,115 --> 00:15:55,820
Below more than a thousand meters
of ice, researchers recovered frozen

290
00:15:55,835 --> 00:15:57,510
material from the ancient ground.

291
00:15:58,035 --> 00:16:01,390
At the time, Camp Century was
already losing its military future.

292
00:16:01,795 --> 00:16:05,330
But the core samples had a future
no one could have fully predicted.

293
00:16:05,855 --> 00:16:09,230
The samples were stored
and moved between institutions.

294
00:16:09,995 --> 00:16:12,720
Some of the deepest material
was long believed lost.

295
00:16:12,735 --> 00:16:16,230
During a twenty nineteen inventory
at the University of Copenhagen,

296
00:16:16,735 --> 00:16:20,960
a curator located the long-missing
subglacial samples and their

297
00:16:20,975 --> 00:16:22,050
original records.

298
00:16:22,555 --> 00:16:25,030
It was evidence from below
the ice sheet itself.

299
00:16:25,475 --> 00:16:29,360
Later studies found traces of ancient
plants and other material associated

300
00:16:29,375 --> 00:16:32,030
with environments very
different from modern Greenland.

301
00:16:32,135 --> 00:16:35,580
The camp that had been built
for a Cold War engineering experiment

302
00:16:35,595 --> 00:16:39,410
had preserved a scientific
record of a far older landscape.

303
00:16:39,855 --> 00:16:44,020
Then came a new way to see the same
abandoned structures — this time

304
00:16:44,035 --> 00:16:48,920
from the air. In April twenty
twenty-four, a NASA aircraft was using

305
00:16:48,935 --> 00:16:51,330
radar to study Greenland's ice.

306
00:16:51,875 --> 00:16:55,520
The instrument detected the buried
structures of Camp Century in

307
00:16:55,535 --> 00:16:56,990
a new level of detail.

308
00:16:57,495 --> 00:17:00,810
Earlier radar surveys had
also detected the base,

309
00:17:00,975 --> 00:17:03,410
but this instrument
revealed more of its layout.

310
00:17:04,115 --> 00:17:06,710
NASA published the images
in November that year.

311
00:17:07,155 --> 00:17:10,070
Not an accessible city filled
with working machinery.

312
00:17:10,495 --> 00:17:14,290
Not a secret missile complex still
operating beneath the glacier.

313
00:17:14,975 --> 00:17:17,860
NASA reported that the structural
remains lay at least one hundred

314
00:17:17,875 --> 00:17:20,930
feet beneath the surface when
described in twenty twenty-four.

315
00:17:21,155 --> 00:17:24,080
That is what makes this ending
different from the one in the Army's

316
00:17:24,095 --> 00:17:28,120
original film. The Army film shows
how people built a city beneath

317
00:17:28,135 --> 00:17:32,080
the snow. The radar shows what
remains after the snow has

318
00:17:32,095 --> 00:17:33,900
continued moving for sixty years.

319
00:17:33,915 --> 00:17:38,420
And the scientific core reveals that
the ice also holds records older

320
00:17:38,435 --> 00:17:41,910
than any nation that ever claimed
a strategic interest in Greenland.

321
00:17:42,295 --> 00:17:44,510
The engineers built an extraordinary city.

322
00:17:44,795 --> 00:17:47,470
They could not make the material
around it stay still.

323
00:17:47,595 --> 00:17:50,680
And the huge missile network
for which the experiment might have

324
00:17:50,695 --> 00:17:52,610
served as a model never existed.

325
00:17:52,875 --> 00:17:55,150
The lasting evidence is
not a hidden arsenal.

326
00:17:55,435 --> 00:17:57,870
It is the film of the men
who built the place,

327
00:17:58,155 --> 00:18:00,510
the measurements showing
how the tunnels changed,

328
00:18:00,655 --> 00:18:03,620
and the structures and samples
that the ice still holds.

329
00:18:03,635 --> 00:18:07,860
The Army tried to turn Greenland's
ice sheet into an enduring military

330
00:18:07,875 --> 00:18:11,540
advantage. What it left behind
was an engineering warning,

331
00:18:11,555 --> 00:18:14,510
buried in the very material
it had hoped to master.

